{#
   Watching-zenoh RFC §5.O — per-backend SCE-MAP marker macros.

   Spec lines 3094-3160 fix the marker syntax per backend:
     - C  / Cpp:   `#line {line} "{file}"` (preprocessor directive)
     - Rust:       BOTH `#[doc = "SCE-MAP: …"]` AND `// SCE-MAP: …`
                   per spec lines 3135-3136 "MUST emit BOTH" — Atomic 1
                   adds the empirical preservation test that rustdoc
                   keeps the `#[doc]` form on every emitted function.
     - Kotlin:     `// SCE-MAP: {file}:{line}`
     - Go:         `//line {file}:{line}` (recognised by `go vet` +
                                           panic stack traces)
     - Python:     `# SCE-MAP: {file}:{line}`

   `loc` is the IR-attached `Option<SourceLocation>` populated by the
   parser at every emission-eligible node (watching-zenoh RFC §5.O
   Atomic 0a, see `sce-build/src/forge/provenance.rs`). The macro
   silently elides when `loc` is None — the codegen pre-emit walker
   (`validate_emission_provenance`) already gates on the populate, so
   a None reaching this point is a parser-site regression caught by
   the `traceability/scxml-line-range-missing` diagnostic, not a
   silent miss [[feedback-silently-broken-hooks]].

   `module_level=true` switches the Rust emission to the inner-attribute
   form `#![doc = "..."]` so a top-of-module marker doesn't collide with
   a `pub` item's own rustdoc. Other backends ignore the flag.

   Atomic 0b lands the macro + ONE invocation per backend at the top
   of the generated state machine source so the infrastructure proves
   end-to-end. Atomic 0c extends to per-state on_entry/on_exit + per-
   transition handlers + forge per-kind body functions; the macro is
   single-source-of-truth so a syntax tweak there edits one place.
#}

{%- macro source_marker(loc, backend, module_level=false) -%}
{%- if loc and loc.line -%}
{%- if backend == "c" or backend == "cpp" -%}
{#- C/Cpp `#line` is a preprocessor directive that remaps line
    numbers for the *rest of the translation unit*. At file scope
    (especially in a `.h` header included by many TUs) this would
    propagate into every consumer's diagnostics. The comment form
    is safe at module scope; Atomic 0c switches to `#line` for
    per-function emission where the redirect is locally scoped to
    the function body. -#}
{%- if module_level %}
// SCE-MAP: {{ loc.file }}:{{ loc.line }}
{%- else %}
#line {{ loc.line }} "{{ loc.file }}"
{%- endif %}
{%- elif backend == "rust" -%}
{%- if module_level %}
#![doc = "SCE-MAP: {{ loc.file }}:{{ loc.line }}"]
// SCE-MAP: {{ loc.file }}:{{ loc.line }}
{%- else %}
#[doc = "SCE-MAP: {{ loc.file }}:{{ loc.line }}"]
// SCE-MAP: {{ loc.file }}:{{ loc.line }}
{%- endif %}
{%- elif backend == "kotlin" %}
// SCE-MAP: {{ loc.file }}:{{ loc.line }}
{%- elif backend == "go" -%}
{#- Go's `//line` directive remaps subsequent line numbers in panic
    stack traces + `go vet` output. Like C/Cpp `#line`, file-scope
    placement would shift every subsequent file position. Use the
    comment form at module level; switch to `//line` for per-
    function emission in Atomic 0c. -#}
{%- if module_level %}
// SCE-MAP: {{ loc.file }}:{{ loc.line }}
{%- else %}
//line {{ loc.file }}:{{ loc.line }}
{%- endif %}
{%- elif backend == "python" %}
# SCE-MAP: {{ loc.file }}:{{ loc.line }}
{%- endif %}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
